# Paddle's default payment link is https://justglide.app/checkout, because justglide.app is the
# only domain Paddle approved for this account. The page itself lives on the Glide proxy, and a
# custom domain for that proxy is a paid Specific feature we deliberately do not buy. A 200
# rewrite is a reverse proxy, so the buyer stays on the approved domain while Netlify fetches
# the page from the proxy. Status 200, NOT 301: a redirect would move the buyer to an
# unapproved domain and Paddle would reject the checkout.
# ORDER MATTERS, exact first. Netlify takes the first matching rule, and "/checkout/*" also
# matches bare "/checkout" with an empty splat, which forwards to ".../checkout/" with a
# trailing slash. Fastify routes strictly and 404s that. Measured, not guessed:
#   {"message":"Route GET:/checkout/ not found","error":"Not Found","statusCode":404}
# The splat rule below is still needed, for /checkout/fonts/*.woff2.
/checkout    https://black-yak.spcf.app/checkout         200
/checkout/*  https://black-yak.spcf.app/checkout/:splat  200

# Same reverse-proxy trick, for a different reason: the HTML `download` attribute is IGNORED on a
# CROSS-ORIGIN link. Pointing the buttons straight at black-yak.spcf.app therefore dropped the
# attribute, the browser treated the click as a navigation to an attachment, and the user got a
# Save-As panel asking them to name the file instead of the one-click, already-named download every
# other site gives (user report 2026-08-01). Routing it through justglide.app makes the link
# SAME-ORIGIN, so `download` is honoured and the file lands in Downloads under its own name.
# The proxy still sees the request, so the download counter and the active-release lookup are
# unaffected.
/download/mac  https://black-yak.spcf.app/download/mac  200

# ONE ADDRESS PER PAGE (SEO, 2026-10-18). Netlify serves faq.html at /faq on its own, which left
# every page reachable at two addresses, with canonicals, links and the sitemap disagreeing about
# which one was real. The clean form is now the only one: canonicals, og:url, internal links and
# sitemap.xml all use it, and the .html form 301s here. The ! is required, because faq.html
# exists as a file and Netlify skips unforced rules that shadow a real file. No rule may point a
# clean path back at its .html file (the old "/community /community.html 200" rewrites were
# removed for that reason): Netlify already resolves /community to community.html by itself.
# /index.html is deliberately left alone: its canonical already says /, and the home page is the
# one place a redirect mistake would take the whole site down.
/faq.html              /faq               301!
/privacy.html          /privacy           301!
/creator.html          /creator           301!
/community.html        /community         301!
/pricing.html          /pricing           301!
/feature-request.html  /feature-request   301!
/compare/wispr-flow.html     /compare/wispr-flow      301!
/compare/superwhisper.html   /compare/superwhisper    301!
/compare/lemon.html          /compare/lemon           301!

# Repo files that are not part of the website. The site publishes from the repo root, so these
# were publicly downloadable. Forced 404s hide them without moving any file.
/BRAND_MEMORY.md     /404.html  404!
/package.json        /404.html  404!
/package-lock.json   /404.html  404!
/test-privacy.js     /404.html  404!
/Glide_macOS.dmg     /404.html  404!

/r/*  /r/index.html  200
